module DASHI.Physics.YangMills.BalabanA1RescaledConstrainedGaussianScaleUniformFloorExact where

------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ROW A1, NORMALISATION HALF:  THE RESCALED CONSTRAINED GAUSSIAN NORM,
--   AND A PROOF THAT IT IS FORCED.
--
-- PRIMARY SOURCES
--
-- Tadeusz Bałaban, "Renormalization Group Approach to Lattice Gauge Field
-- Theories. I.  Generation of Effective Actions in a Small Field
-- Approximation and a Coupling Constant Renormalization in Four Dimensions",
-- Communications in Mathematical Physics 109 (1987), 249--301.
-- DOI: 10.1007/BF01215223.  Source locators: the block-spin normalisation of
-- the fluctuation field in Sections 2--3, and the constrained Gaussian
-- integral of Section 4.
--
-- Tadeusz Bałaban, "Large Field Renormalization II", Communications in
-- Mathematical Physics 122 (1989), 355--392.  DOI: 10.1007/BF01238433.
-- Relationship: the scale-uniform small-field regime the floor must survive.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE ADDS.
--
-- Addendum J.4 of the obstruction report (module
-- `BalabanA1ReferenceCoercivityScaleUniformityExact`) established a NEGATIVE
-- fact: the repository's A1 surface fixes the reference coercivity constant
-- proportional to the running coupling,
--
--       cReference_j  =  gamma * beta_j ,
--
-- and under asymptotic freedom `beta_j` contracts, so in the UNRESCALED
-- (dimensionful) tangent norm no scale-uniform positive floor can exist.  That
-- module closed with the sentence "naming that norm, and the constants in it,
-- is the remaining A1 analysis".  This module does the naming, and proves that
-- the naming is not a matter of taste.
--
-- 1.  THE NORM.  The rescaled (dimensionless) squared fluctuation norm is the
--     coupling-weighted one,
--
--       ||x||^2_{j,rescaled}  =  weight_j * ||x||^2 ,
--
--     with `weight_j` the SAME block-spin/coupling normalisation that appears
--     in `cReference_j`.  `couplingScaledIsUniformAfterRescaling` proves that
--     in this norm the literal coupling-scaled coercivity hypothesis is
--     *exactly* a scale-uniform floor with the scale-independent constant
--     `gamma`, and `uniformRescaledFloorIsCouplingScaled` proves the converse,
--     so the two statements are equivalent, not merely comparable.
--
-- 2.  THE PERTURBATION BUDGET SURVIVES THE RESCALING.  A RELATIVE budget
--     `penalty <= theta * cReference_j * ||x||^2` is, in the rescaled norm,
--     `penalty <= (theta*gamma) * ||x||^2_{j,rescaled}`: the ratio `theta` is
--     rescaling invariant.  `rescaledRelativeCoercivity` therefore delivers
--     the full constrained Hessian floor `(1-theta)*gamma`, uniform in the
--     scale -- the mechanism of `BalabanRelativeHessianCoercivity`, now with a
--     constant that does not contract.
--
-- 3.  THE NORM IS FORCED, NOT CHOSEN.  This is the substantive part.  Suppose
--     the literal pairing is two-sidedly comparable to the coupling weight at
--     one witness state with positive norm,
--
--       (gamma * weight_j) ||x||^2  <=  <x, H_j x>  <=  (Gamma * weight_j) ||x||^2 ,
--
--     and suppose some other weight family `w_j` is proposed.  Then
--
--       * a scale-uniform positive floor `c` in the `w`-norm forces
--         `c * w_j <= Gamma * weight_j`   (`admissibleWeightBelowCouplingWeight`),
--       * a scale-uniform finite ceiling `C` in the `w`-norm forces
--         `gamma * weight_j <= C * w_j`   (`admissibleWeightAboveCouplingWeight`).
--
--     So any admissible rescaling weight is comparable to the coupling weight
--     from both sides (`rescalingWeightIsForced`): up to fixed constants there
--     is exactly one dimensionless norm in which A1 can be stated.  This rules
--     out repairing J.4 by any other choice of normalisation.
--
-- 4.  THE CONTRAST, ON ONE MODEL.  `Model` below is a one-dimensional exact
--     rational instance with dyadic weight `weight_j = 2^{-j}`.  On literally
--     the same data:
--       * `modelNoUnrescaledFloorAboveOneThousandth` -- no floor above 1/1000
--         in the unrescaled norm (the J.4 phenomenon), and
--       * `modelRescaledUniformFloor` -- floor 1 at every scale in the
--         rescaled norm.
--     So the failure is a property of the norm, not of the physics.
--
-- 5.  IT FITS THE EXISTING SURFACE.  `RelativeHessianCoercivityData` carries a
--     scale-free `normSq : State -> Bound`.  `pairedNormSq` shows the rescaled
--     norm is expressible there exactly when the state carries its own scale,
--     and `pairedScaleUniformFloor` inhabits the scale-free coercivity shape
--     with one scale-independent constant.
--
-- WHAT THIS IS NOT.  It does not produce `gamma`, `Gamma` or `weight_j` for
-- Yang-Mills: the literal Wilson + reduced Faddeev-Popov + Haar constrained
-- Hessian is still the physical leaf, and Row A stays open.  What it removes
-- is the normalisation ambiguity that J.4 exposed: the target inequality
-- `c_ref >= c_0 > 0` now has a unique meaning.
--
-- Exact rational arithmetic; no postulate, no hole, no receipt, no pragma.
-- Checked with `agda --safe`.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

open import Agda.Builtin.Nat using (Nat)
open import Agda.Builtin.Unit using (⊤; tt)
open import Agda.Builtin.Equality using (_≡_; refl)
open import Data.Empty using (⊥)
open import Data.Integer.Base using (+_)
open import Data.Product using (_×_; _,_; proj₁; proj₂)
open import Data.Rational.Base as ℚ
  using (ℚ; 0ℚ; 1ℚ; _+_; _-_; _*_; -_; _≤_; _<_; _/_)
import Data.Rational.Properties as ℚP
open import Relation.Binary.PropositionalEquality
  using (sym; trans; cong; cong₂; subst; subst₂)
open import Relation.Nullary.Decidable using (toWitness)

open import DASHI.Physics.YangMills.CompactLieProofLevel
